# Version control and CI
.git
.github
.gitignore

# Python artifacts
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.venv/
venv/

# Secrets -- must never enter the build context, let alone the image.
# Credentials belong only in a `docker run -e` / --env-file at runtime
# (see src/secrets.py's policy). This mirrors .gitignore's own rule.
#
# `.env*` rather than `.env` + `*.env`: neither of those matches
# `.env.staging` or `.env.production`, so the real per-environment
# credential files were entering the build context and being baked
# into the image by `COPY . .` -- exactly what the paragraph above
# says must never happen. They were harmless only while empty.
.env
.env*
*.env

# fidelity-api writes these into the working directory, and two of them
# are credential-equivalent: Fidelity*.json is the Playwright
# storage_state for an AUTHENTICATED brokerage session (cookies +
# localStorage, plaintext), and fidelity_trace*.zip is a Playwright
# trace that records .fill() arguments -- the username and password in
# cleartext. `COPY . .` would bake either straight into the image.
# Mirrors .gitignore; see there for the full reasoning.
Fidelity.json
Fidelity_*.json
fidelity_trace*.zip
Statements/
Portfolio_Positions*.csv

# fidelity_recon.py's traffic dumps -- captured authenticated-session
# traffic (tokens, account numbers). Written outside the repo by default;
# excluded here for the same reason as the files above. Mirrors .gitignore.
.fidelity_recon/
recon_*.json

# Local persistent state and user-supplied data -- these are meant to
# be bind-mounted at runtime (see the Dockerfile's VOLUME and
# docker-compose.yml), never baked into the image.
state/
data/
output/
*.db

# Editor/OS cruft
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
